---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Header from '../components/Header.astro';
import Footer from '../components/Footer.astro';
import Masthead from '../components/Masthead.astro';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="open-bench — model royale"
  description="Model Royale: a weekly elimination-style coding tournament between every major LLM, run on the open-bench harness."
>
  <Header subtitle="model royale" />

  <Masthead
    title="model royale"
    subtitle="A weekly coding tournament between every major LLM. One task. Same blank repo. Last model standing."
  />

  <article class="prose about-prose">
    <p>
      <strong>Model Royale</strong> is the flagship format running on top of open-bench
      &mdash; a weekly elimination-style competition where every major coding LLM faces
      the same task in the same blank repo. Hidden tests gate first; the models then
      score each other; the cheapest win ties. Each week one task, one set of artifacts,
      one ranked review. The loser of the round is eliminated from the lineup; a
      challenger gets called up. Repeat indefinitely.
    </p>

    <p>
      open-bench itself is the harness underneath: the framework that drops a spec
      into a worktree, drives each model through opencode, captures transcripts and
      diffs, runs the hidden test battery, fans the implementations out to every
      judge, and aggregates the results into a single review. Model Royale is one
      thing you can run on top of that &mdash; the weekly broadcast format with a
      narrative arc, eliminations, and a public scoreboard. You could run a flat
      n-way comparison instead, or a single-model regression, or a private bake-off
      between two of your candidates. The harness doesn't care.
    </p>

    <p>
      The format exists because picking a coding model from leaderboards is a bad
      time. They benchmark one-shot prompts, report pass@1 on synthetic problems,
      and update on a vendor's calendar. None of that survives contact with a real
      agent loop where wall-clock, cost, and code quality matter as much as whether
      it solves the problem. Model Royale is built around what you actually need to
      know: did it pass the hidden tests, how much did it cost, how fast was it,
      and what did its peers think of the code. Same task, same harness, same
      week. Apples-to-apples or it doesn't ship.
    </p>

    <p>
      The current round is on the <a href="/">home page</a>. Past rounds, transcripts,
      diffs, and full per-judge rubrics are committed under
      <a href="https://github.com/anfocic/open-bench/tree/main/builds">builds/</a> and
      <a href="https://github.com/anfocic/open-bench/tree/main/results">results/</a>.
      For the harness internals, the scoring math, and forking the framework for
      your own format, see <a href="/about">about</a> and
      <a href="https://github.com/anfocic/open-bench/blob/main/ABOUT.md">ABOUT.md</a>.
    </p>
  </article>

  <Footer />
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .about-prose { max-width: 68ch; margin-top: 8px; }
  .about-prose p { margin: 14px 0; }
</style>
